#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116468 :tutorial/content "Consider the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\cot x = 0, \\qquad 0 < x < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{1}{0} = \\textrm{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\cot x = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\cos x &=0\\[5pt]\nx &= \\arccos 0 \\[5pt]\nx &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=\\dfrac\\pi2$ and $x=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cea72c7e-2081-5be6-b8fa-01b6cc7d3066" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2569 :tutorial/title "Equations Where the Cotangent Function Equals Zero"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116515 :tutorial/content "To reduce a rational expression to its lowest terms, we need to factor the numerator and denominator and then cancel out the common factors.\n\nFor example, to simplify the rational expression $\\dfrac{2x}{2+6x},$ we cancel out a factor of $2$ from the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{2+6x} & = \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2(1+3x)} & = \\\\ \\frac{x}{1+3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, however, we can have more complicated expressions which require **polynomial factorization** in order to be simplified.\n\nNo need to panic though! The main strategy is always the same: factor the numerator and denominator, and then cancel out common factors.\n\nFor example, to simplify the rational expression $\\dfrac{x^2-4}{x+2},$ we factor the difference of squares in the numerator and then cancel out a common factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{x+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)(x-2)}{x+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(x+2)}(x-2)}{\\cancel{x+2}} & = \\\\ x-2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4e98d51f-c45b-52ae-9a50-c3d9b4285aa4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3374 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121006 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x}\n$$\n\nIf we try to evaluate the limit by directly substituting $x=0$, we get a confusing result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)\\frac{x^{3} - 2x}{x^{2} + 4x} &= ((0)^{3} - 2(0))/((0)^{2} + 4(0)) = \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 0 0\n$$\n\nis called an **indeterminate form** because we are unable to determine the value of the limit from this expression.\n\nHowever, if we simplify the fraction before evaluating the limit, we obtain a more clear result. To simplify the fraction, we can factor the numerator and denominator and then cancel out any common factors.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x} & = \\dfrac{x(x^2-2)}{x(x+4)} \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{\\cancel{x}(x^2-2)}{\\cancel{x}(x+4)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the limit of the simplified fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x} &= \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{(0)^2-2}{(0)+4} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{-2}{4} \\\\ & = -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "358e387e-1f79-5799-a6bc-d9a94fd46dd9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 246 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121146 :tutorial/content "We can simplify the polynomial below because it contains a pair of like terms:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{3x^2}} +4x \\,{\\color{blue}{-}}\\,{\\color{blue}{2x^2}} +5\n$$\n\nTo simplify, we combine the like terms ($3x^2$ and $-2x^2$) by adding or subtracting their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4x-2{x}^{2}+5 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+4x+5 & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2})+4x+5 & = \\\\ (3-2){x}^{2}+4x+5 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+5 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dcac1959-d802-5641-8736-12d53db273a9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8864 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125737 :tutorial/content "From the formula for the directional derivative, we get that if  $z = f (x, y)$ is a differentiable function and $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector, then\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf'_{\\mathbf u} &= \\nabla f \\cdot \\mathbf u \\[3pt]\n                      &= |\\nabla f| |\\mathbf u| \\cos\\theta\\[3pt]\n                      & = |\\nabla f| \\cos\\theta,\n}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle between the vectors $\\nabla f$ and $\\mathbf u,$ as depicted below.\n\n![](images/50/50c82908d5fd619c1c3c45f2aa3ff26929b8f369233c08d0688a091d2d3c123c.png)\n\n \n\nIt follows that $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is maximized when $\\theta =0.$ In other words, $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is maximized when $\\mathbf u$ and $\\nabla f$ point in the same direction. Thus, the maximum value of $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is $|\\nabla f|.$\n\nA similar argument shows that $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ reaches its minimum value $- |\\nabla f|$ when $\\theta = \\pi;$ that is, when $\\nabla f$ and $\\mathbf u$ point in opposite directions.\n\nAs a result, we get the following theorem:\n\n*A differentiable function $f$ increases most rapidly in the direction of the gradient and decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction to that of its gradient.*\n\nFor functions of more than two variables, the same theorem holds." :tutorial/id #uuid "5e4c2cac-e80c-51dd-8138-681fa98ae521" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2505 :tutorial/title "The Gradient as the Direction of Maximum Increase of a Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125739 :tutorial/content "Consider a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ and suppose that the point $P$ with coordinates $(x_0,y_0, f(x_0,y_0))$ lies on $S.$ We wish to derive a formula for the directional derivative $f'_\\mathbf{u}(x_0,y_0),$ where $\\mathbf u = \\langle u_1, u_2 \\rangle$ is a unit vector.\n\nGeometrically, $f'_{\\mathbf u}(x_0,y_0)$ can be constructed in the following way:\n\n- Define a vertical plane $\\Pi$ that's parallel to $\\mathbf u$ and passes through $P.$\n\n- Define the curve $C$ as the intersection of the surface $S$ and the plane $\\Pi.$\n\n- Then, the directional derivative $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$\n\nThe geometric interpretation of $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is similar to that of the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y.$ The only difference here is that we are not restricting the plane $\\Pi$ to be parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nNow, since $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is tangent to $C$ and is in the direction of $\\mathbf u$ at $P,$ the vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf v = \\langle u_1, u_2, f'_{\\mathbf u}\\rangle\n$$\n\nis tangent to our surface $S$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/59/59cb8fa410b9194c298d8f477ab0d0cff3e27765bc45c14cb9b5ea9210288184.png)\n\nRecall that $\\langle f_x (x_0,y_0), f_y(x_0,y_0), -1\\rangle$ is known to be a normal vector to the surface at $P.$ Since this vector must be perpendicular to $\\mathbf v,$ the dot product of the two vectors must be zero, which means that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\langle u_1, u_2, f'_{\\mathbf u}\\rangle \\cdot \\langle f_x (x_0,y_0), f_y(x_0,y_0), -1\\rangle &=0 \\[5pt]\nu_1 f_x (x_0,y_0) + u_2 f_y (x_0,y_0) - f'_{\\mathbf u} &=0 \\[5pt]\nf'_{\\mathbf u} = \\nabla f (x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\mathbf u. }\n$$\n\nWe can interpret this formula as the projection of $\\nabla f (x_0,y_0)$ onto the direction of $\\mathbf u,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/82/82e7dece2597e15172600ff598090241facb834a7248ed54286fa777c25a6ebd.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "baa62c26-a25a-5293-a28f-128d45e61d68" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2509 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula For the Directional Derivative"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125815 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that the gradient of a function $f(x,y)$ is \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2xy-3x^2) \\ \\mathbf i + (x^2-2y) \\ \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\n \n\nHow can we reconstruct the function $f(x,y)$ knowing only its gradient?\n\nSince the components of $\\nabla f$ must be the first-order partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y$ respectively, we know that \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} =  2xy-3x^2,   \\qquad \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x^2-2y.}\n$$\n\nIntegrating  $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ as a constant, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\int \\left(2xy-3x^2\\right) \\textrm d  x = x^2y -x^3 +g(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(y)$ is an arbitrary function of $y.$ Intuitively, $g(y)$ contains all the terms that would vanish when differentiated with respect to $x.$\n\nNow, differentiating the above with respect to $y$ gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} = x^2 + g'(y).\n$$\n\nSo, equating the two expressions for $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{ \nx^2 + g'(y) &= x^2-2y\\ \n        g'(y)  & = -2y.\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle g(y)=\\int(-2y) \\ \\textrm d y =  -y^2 +C.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^2y -x^3-y^2 +C .\n$$\n\nNote that the above procedure is symmetric with respect to $x$ and $y.$ We could have started by integrating $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y$ and then differentiating with respect to $x.$ The final result would have been the same." :tutorial/id #uuid "3d2fa632-58e8-5d68-a8e2-fb8822d211c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4256 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125847 :tutorial/content "Up to now, we've been using the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{r} & ={f}_{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{f}_{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {f}_{\\theta } & =-r{f}_{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r{f}_{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can treat the above as a system of equations in the \"unknowns\" $f_x$ and $f_y.$ Solving this system gives explicit expressions for $f_x$ and $f_y$ in terms of functions of $r$ and $\\theta.$\n\nFor example, suppose we want an explicit formula for $f_x.$ We can eliminate $f_y$ by multiplying the first equation by $r\\cos\\theta$ and the second equation by $\\sin\\theta$ to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r{f}_{r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =r{f}_{x}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +r{f}_{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ {f}_{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-r{f}_{x}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +r{f}_{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first gives\n\n$$\nr f_r \\cos\\theta - f_\\theta \\sin\\theta = r f_x(\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta),\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\nf_x = f_r \\cos\\theta - \\dfrac1r f_\\theta \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe can use a similar procedure to write $f_y$ in terms of functions of $r$ and $\\theta.$ Our results as summarized below:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} &= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial r} \\cos \\theta\n\t\t\t\t\t\t-\\dfrac{1}{r} \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin \\theta\\[1ex]\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial r} \\sin \\theta\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ \\dfrac{1}{r} \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta}\\cos \\theta\n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e3237863-f115-5396-a03f-fd481d101761" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5358 :tutorial/title "Differentiating of a Function Given in Polar Coordinates With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130327 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to compute the tangent plane to the level surface $f (x, y, z) = k,$ where $k$ is a constant, at some point $\\mathbf x_0 = (x_0, y_0, z_0).$ \n\nAs an example, consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = x^2 +y^2+z.\n$$\n\n \n\nSuppose that we wish to find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=  (1,1, 2).$ \n\nEvaluating $f$ at the point $(1,1,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = (1)^2 +(1)^2+2 = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the equation of our level surface is\n\n$$\nx^2 +y^2+z = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the equation of the tangent plane to this surface at $(1,1,2),$ we can use the following result:\n\n  *Given a hypersurface $w = f(x,y,z)$ and a point $\\mathbf x_0 = (x_0,y_0,z_0)$ that lies in the domain of $f$, the gradient $\\nabla \\! f (\\mathbf x_0)$ is normal to the tangent plane of the level surface $f (x, y, z) = k$ at the point $\\mathbf x_0$*. \n\n![](images/11/1140e80fbb2efd684311cb7625e5b0be361d6fb951036253f57b9e51234de3ee.png)\n\n       \n\nWe can now find the equation of the tangent plane to our level surface.\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $\\mathbf x_0 = (1,1,2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}\\,i+{f}_{y}\\,j+{f}_{z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,2) & =2(1)\\text{ }i+2(1)\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =2\\text{ }i+2\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally,  we write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle & = 0 \\[3pt]\n\\nabla f(1,1,2) \\cdot \\langle x-1, \\: y-1, \\: z-2 \\rangle & = 0 \\[3pt]\n\\langle 2,\\:2,\\:1\\rangle\\cdot \\langle x-1, y-1, z-2\\rangle &= 0\\[3pt]\n      2(x-1)+ 2(y-1) + (z-2) & =0\\\n      2x +2y +z -6&=0   \n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7f7db883-9d6e-5a67-97d4-20274467a1e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2500 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130340 :tutorial/content "To raise the product of two numbers to a power, we can raise each number to that power, and then multiply. This is the **power of product rule for exponents**. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $(5 \\times 3)^4$ by applying the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n(5\\times 3)^4=5^4\\times 3^4\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power of product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5\\times 3)^{4} & = \\\\ \\underbrace{(5\\times 3)\\times (5\\times 3)\\times (5\\times 3)\\times (5\\times 3)}_{4\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ \\underbrace{(5\\times 5\\times 5\\times 5)}_{4\\text{ copies}}\\times \\underbrace{(3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3)}_{4\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {5}^{4}\\times {3}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "79b0e407-5948-563e-a8af-5dbcd3ab59f3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3272 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130434 :tutorial/content "A **polynomial identity** is an equation where both sides represent the same polynomial even though they are written differently.\n\nSome of the most important polynomial identities are as follows:\n\n$$\n(x + a)^{2} = x^{2} + 2ax + a^{2}; (x - a)^{2} = x^{2} - 2ax + a^{2}; (x - a)(x + a) = x^{2} - a^{2}; x^{2} + (a + b)x + ab = (x + a)(x + b)\n$$\n\nTo prove that a polynomial equation is an identity, we start with one side of the equation and then show that it's equivalent to the other side using algebraic manipulation:\n\nFor example, we can show that\n\n$$\n(x-5)^2+10x = x^2 + 25\n$$\n\nis a polynomial identity by performing the following steps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 5)^{2} + 10x &=  \\\\\nStep 1:\\begin{vmatrix}(x^{2} - 2 \\cdot x \\cdot 5 + 5^{2}) + 10x &= \\\\ Step 2:\\end{vmatrix}(x^{2} - 10x + 25) + 10x|= \\\\\nStep 3:\\begin{vmatrix}x^{2} + (-10x + 10x) + 25 &= \\\\ Step 4:\\end{vmatrix}x^{2} + 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fe34efb7-658f-5a0c-9029-1abfb59172e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5976 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130458 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to evaluate the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1\\int_{x^2}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1}\\,\\dfrac{e^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\textrm{d}z\\, \\textrm{d}y\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFor this repeated integral, the integration domain is the type I region \n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\:  x^2 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas illustrated below.\n\n![](images/54/54bdc97100c19657d261f35123eec8a4d2d927f0d92073462ae6e9335aef0921.png)\n\nThe expression '$\\,\\textrm{d}z\\, \\textrm{d}y\\, \\textrm{d}x$' means we should integrate first with respect to $z,$ then with respect to $y,$ and lastly with respect to $x.$ Let's see what happens when we attempt to integrate in this order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot [z]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot proceed any further because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of $\\dfrac{e^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}.$\n\nTo resolve this issue, we first note that the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane is the type I plane region $D$ shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f681a48038d51cdb592b265e7f482aa03fffb2d1235df02b38390083fe73f3a.png)\n\nThis region can also be represented as the type II plane region.\n\n![](images/89/89836e63d7f184624334daef7c50239dc818324f24071118aa41a2a1ca5accb6.png)\n\nUsing set notation, $D$'s representation as a type II plane region is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate this integral by keeping the inner integral the same and swapping the order of integration in the middle and outer integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate this integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}[z]_{0}^{1}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot [x]_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\sqrt{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{y}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =e-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote the following:\n\n- In this lesson, we'll learn how to compute triple integrals by changing the representation of the projection $D$ of $R$ from a type I plane region to type II (and vice-versa).\n\n- It's also possible to change the spatial (i.e., three-dimensional) representation of $R$ from type I to type II, type I to type III, type II to type III, etc. This is slightly more involved, so we'll leave that discussion for a separate lesson.\n\n$$\n%\\eqalign{\n%\\int_0^1\\int_0^1\\int_{{x}}^{1} \\,6z e^{zy^2}\\,\\textrm{d}y\\, \\textrm{d}x\\, \\, \\textrm{d}z &= \\int_0^1\\int_0^1\\int_{{0}}^{y} \\,6z e^{zy^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x\\, \\textrm{d}y\\, \\, \\textrm{d}z\\\n%&= \\int_0^1\\int_0^1 \\,6z e^{zy^2} \\left[x\\right]_{{0}}^{y}\\, \\textrm{d}y\\, \\, \\textrm{d}z\\\n%&= \\int_0^1\\int_0^1 \\,6z ye^{zy^2} \\, \\textrm{d}y\\, \\, \\textrm{d}z\\\n%&=  \\int_0^1 \\, 3 \\left[e^{zy^2}\\right]_0^1 \\,  \\, \\textrm{d}z\\\n%&=  \\int_0^1 \\, 3 \\left(e^z-1\\right)  \\, \\textrm{d}z\\\n%&= 3e-6.}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0583a558-7d9d-59a9-b3df-1804f157fe28" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7287 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130481 :tutorial/content "As we know already, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).\n\nThe same rules apply to numerical expressions with zero and negative exponents. To demonstrate, let's compute the value of\n\n$$\n(-3)^0 +2^2 \\div 2.\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that $\\left(-3\\right)^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-3)}^{0}+{2}^{2}÷2 & = \\\\ 1+{2}^{2}÷2 & = \\\\ 1+4÷2 & = \\\\ 1+2 & = \\\\ 3 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e2d29fa0-8bcb-537b-bed9-91a9ff109764" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8061 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135015 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to evaluate the expression $\\sqrt x + 3$ for the value $x=9.$ This type of expression is called a **radical expression** because it contains the radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}.$ Remember that $\\sqrt x$ means \"the positive square root of the number $x.$\" \n\nTo evaluate this expression at the given value, we replace $x$ with $9$ in the given expression and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x}+3 & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9}+3 & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{3}^{2}}+3 & = \\\\ 3+3 & = \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** You may have heard somewhere that $\\sqrt{9} = \\pm 3,$ because $(-3)^2=9$ and $(3)^2 = 9.$ However, when we write down $\\sqrt{9},$ we *always* mean \"the positive square root of $9$\".\n\nIf we want the negative square root of $9$, we write $-\\sqrt{9} = -3.$ Sometimes, we want both, in which case we would write $\\pm\\sqrt{9}=\\pm 3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4457c20d-88c9-5028-8390-390579fa7229" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1610 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135017 :tutorial/content "It's often more useful to translate from the recursive formula to an explicit one. So how do we find the explicit formula for the geometric sequence defined recursively as\n\n$$\na_{n+1} ={\\color{red}\\dfrac 1 4} a_n,\\, \\qquad a_1 = {\\color{blue}2},\\, \\qquad n\\geq 1?\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1,\n$$\n\nso we just need to determine the first term $a_1$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $a_1={\\color{blue}2}.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n+1} & =r\\cdot {a}_{n} \\\\ {a}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the above, we see that ${\\color{red}r} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac 1 4}.$\n\nNow we substitute $a_1={\\color{blue}2}$ and $r={\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\na_n &= a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1} \\\n&= {\\color{blue}2} \\cdot \\left( {\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{4}} \\right)^{n-1}\t\t\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n=2 \\cdot \\left( \\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d326c47a-d9db-5669-be98-4bee64971fd7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1671 :tutorial/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135192 :tutorial/content "It's often more useful to translate from the recursive formula to an explicit one. So how do we find the explicit formula for the arithmetic sequence defined recursively as\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n + {\\color{red}1},\\, \\quad a_1 = {\\color{blue}6},\\, \\quad n\\geq 1?\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d, \\quad n \\geq 1,\n$$\n\nso we just need to determine the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $a_1={\\color{blue}6},$ and we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+1 \\\\  & =6+1 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =7-6 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $a_1 = {\\color{blue}6}$ and $d={\\color{red}1}$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\na_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\[3pt]\n&= {\\color{blue}6} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}1}) \\[3pt]\n& =6 + n-1\\[3pt]\n&=n+5\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n=n+5, \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c6c5b9f8-4c71-5c1f-8841-3f22646e5074" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8914 :tutorial/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139599 :tutorial/content "We can use unit conversions to convert from one set of units to another.\n\nFor instance, the speed of sound is approximately $0.34$ kilometers per second. What is this in meters per second? We can find out using the following two steps.\n\n** Step 1: Create a conversion formula.**\n\nWe are given the units $\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{s}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{s}\\cdot\\, \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\textrm{km}$ with $\\textrm{m}.$ To get rid of $\\textrm{km},$ we can put $\\textrm{km}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}} }{s}\\cdot \\dfrac{?}{\\cancel{ \\textrm{km} } } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThen, to introduce $\\textrm{m},$ we can put $\\textrm{m}$ in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}} }{s}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m} }{\\cancel{ \\textrm{km} } } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{s}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m} }{\\textrm{km} } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Use the conversion formula.**\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The speed that we wish to convert is $0.34 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{s}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ meters per kilometer.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.34\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}} & =0.34\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{km}} \\\\  & =(0.34\\cdot 1\\,000)\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}} \\\\  & =340\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the speed of sound is $340$ meters per second." :tutorial/id #uuid "d1f56c71-9cb3-5a5b-881c-bfbad588ca7f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1404 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139625 :tutorial/content "When we divide two whole numbers using long division, we often get a remainder. In this lesson, we will learn how to use long division to express the quotient and remainder as a decimal.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $4 \\div 5$. We start by writing down $4\\div 5$ using long division notation:\n\n| $\\,\\,0$$5$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{\\color{red}4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNotice that $\\color{red}4$ is less than the divisor $5$. But we can proceed further by adding a $\\color{blue}0$ after the decimal point in the dividend. We also add a decimal point in the quotient, as shown below:\n\n| $\\,0 {\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}}$$5$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}} {\\color{blue}0}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow we complete the division, as usual, stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0$:\n\n| $\\,0 {\\,.\\,} 8$$5$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\,. \\,} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4 \\div 5 = 0.8\\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "afe71e1c-22d6-554f-a68a-e1f845934e3f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1961 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139628 :tutorial/content "We can use our knowledge of rounding to estimate multi-digit division. To illustrate, let's estimate the value of\n\n$$\n960 \\div 64\n$$\n\nby rounding the divisor to the nearest ten.\n\nThe divisor is $64$, and rounding to the nearest ten we have\n\n$$\n64 \\rightarrow 60 \\,\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of\n\n$$\n960 \\div 60\n$$\n\nwhich we write as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{960}{60}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the fraction, we can divide the numerator and denominator by $10$ to remove the zeros from the end of both numbers:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{960 \\div 10}{60 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{96}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, we just need to compute\n\n$$\n96 \\div 6 \\,\n$$\n\nWe use long division, as follows:\n\n| $\\,1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6$$6$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$3$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $960 \\div 64$ is approximately $16$." :tutorial/id #uuid "93277a59-d17c-512e-83f8-ca818a5c68ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2016 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139629 :tutorial/content "We can use the standard algorithm for division to divide three-digit-numbers by two-digit numbers.\n\nFor example, what is the value of $528 \\div 24$?\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $24$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we would consider the hundreds, but $24$ doesn't go into $\\color{red}5$. So instead, we consider the hundreds and tens.\nNotice that $24$ goes ${\\color{blue}2}$ times into ${\\color{red}52}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}2$$24$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}5} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}2} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\,\\downarrow$$4$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $24 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 48$Subtract: ${\\color{red}52} - 48 = 4$Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $24$ goes ${\\color{blue}2}$ times into ${\\color{red}48}$. So, we get:\n\n| $2$$\\color{blue}2$$24$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\color{red}4$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\phantom{0}$$0$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $24 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 48$Subtract: ${\\color{red}48} - 48 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n528 \\div 24 = 22 \\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "629d52ca-d952-5849-b177-b6bfdc40fdbc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2017 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139630 :tutorial/content "We can use the standard algorithm for division to divide four-digit numbers by two-digit numbers.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use the algorithm to find the value of $4,564 \\div 14$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $14$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the thousands and hundreds. Notice that $14$ goes ${\\color{blue}3}$ times into ${\\color{red}45}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}3$$14$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}5} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$3$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $14 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 42$Subtract: ${\\color{red}45} - 42 = 3$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, notice that $14$ goes ${\\color{blue}2}$ times into ${\\color{red}36}$. So, we get:\n\n| $3$$\\color{blue}2$$14$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{red}6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$8$$\\color{lightgray}4$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $14 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 28$Subtract: ${\\color{red}36} - 28 = 8$Bring down: $4$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $14$ goes ${\\color{blue}6}$ times into ${\\color{red}84}$. So, we get:\n\n| $3$$2$$\\color{blue}6$$14$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$3$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$\\color{red}8$$\\color{red}4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$4$$0$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}6$Multiply: $14 \\times {\\color{blue}6} = 84$Subtract: ${\\color{red}84} - 84 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, and the remainder is $0$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4,564 \\div 14 = 326\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a01748ae-3711-5186-b932-0abcadf17bdf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2018 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139631 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we need to consider the thousands, hundreds, *and* tens digits in the first step when applying the standard algorithm.\n\nTo illustrate, let's calculate $1,967 \\div 28$. We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $28$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNotice that $28$ does not go into $19$. So, we need to consider the thousands, hundreds, *and* tens.\n\nNow, we see that $28$ goes ${\\color{blue}7}$ times into ${\\color{red}196}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}7$$28$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}1} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}9} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}6} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$9$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}7$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}7$Multiply: $28 \\times {\\color{blue}7} = 196$Subtract: ${\\color{red}196} - 196 = 0$Bring down: $7$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $28$ goes ${\\color{blue}0}$ times into ${\\color{red}7}$. So $\\color{red}7$ is the remainder and we get:\n\n| $7$$\\color{blue}0$$28$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$9$$6$$0$$\\color{red}7$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1,967 \\div 28 = 70\\,\\text{R}\\,7 \\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ae3eca76-9e6c-501c-b2a0-a81a343282ea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2019 :tutorial/title "Considering Thousands, Hundreds, and Tens at the First Step"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139633 :tutorial/content "We can use the standard algorithm to divide three-digit decimals by two-digit decimals.\n\nAs an example, let's solve the following division problem:\n\n$$\n92.4 \\div 22\n$$\n\nWe start by writing down our problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $22$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\: \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$22$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process, as usual, we get:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!\\!. 2$$22$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\,. \\,} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$8$$4$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$4$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n92.4 \\div 22 = 4.2\\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a58475ef-d328-57a5-b8dd-05ca0670a7f5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2042 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139634 :tutorial/content "Using the standard algorithm requires the divisor to be a whole number. However, we can often manipulate a division problem using our understanding of fractions to give an equivalent problem where the standard algorithm *can* be applied.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following division problem:\n\n$$\n8.82 \\div 2.5\n$$\n\nWe cannot apply the standard algorithm because the divisor $(2.5)$ is not a whole number.\n\nHowever, if we express this division problem as a fraction, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8.82}{2.5}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to create a whole number in the denominator. To do this, we make an equivalent fraction by multiplying both the numerator and denominator by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8.82 \\times 10}{2.5 \\times 10} = \\dfrac{88.2}{25}\n$$\n\nSo, instead of our original problem, we can now solve the equivalent problem\n\n$$\n88.2 \\div 25\n$$\n\nLet's write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $25$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}8 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\: {\\:\\phantom{\\mid \\mid}} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid \\mid}} {\\phantom{\\mid \\mid}} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$25$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}8 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process, as usual, we get:\n\n| $3$$\\!\\!. 5$$2$$8$$\\!25$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\,. \\,}2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$5$$1$$3$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$5$$7$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$0$$2$$0$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n8.82 \\div 2.5 = 3.528 \\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6d6cf6a9-c698-5d88-8810-f99d9e64c69c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2043 :tutorial/title "Dividing Decimals by Decimals Using the Standard Algorithm"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139643 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nt^4+4t^2+3.\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is a quartic, but it can also be viewed as a quadratic \"in disguise.\" To see this, notice that if we introduce a new variable $z=t^2,$ then we can rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}+3 & = \\\\ ({t}^{2})^{2}+4({t}^{2})+3 & = \\\\ {z}^{2}+4z+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}+4z+3 & = \\\\ (z+1)(z+3) & = \\\\ ({t}^{2}+1)({t}^{2}+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $t^4+4t^2+3$ factors into $(t^2+1)(t^2+3).$ We can double-check this result by multiplying it out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({t}^{2}+1)({t}^{2}+3) & = \\\\ {t}^{2}({t}^{2}+3)+1({t}^{2}+3) & = \\\\ {t}^{4}+3{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}+3 & = \\\\ {t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}+3 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7d740bed-d490-55c4-b311-075223236921" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2961 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139661 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to simplify the following expression: \n\n$$\n2+\\dfrac{5}{2}x -5 + \\dfrac{7}{2}x.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe begin by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{5}{2}x+\\frac{7}{2}x)+(2-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{5}{2}x+\\frac{7}{2}x)+(2-5) & = \\\\ (\\frac{5+7}{2})x+(-3) & = \\\\ \\frac{12}{2}x-3 & = \\\\ 6x-3 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the simplified expression is $6x-3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7ef8a0d2-a9fe-57f8-b3fc-7b69cbb3a818" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8040 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139725 :tutorial/content "The **difference of squares formula** states the product of the sum and the difference of two monomials is equal to the difference of their squares:\n\n$$\n(a + b)(a - b) = a^2 - b^2\n$$\n\nWe can check that this result is correct by expanding the binomial in the usual way, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t({\\color{blue}a} + {\\color{red}b})(a - b) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t{\\color{blue}a}(a - b) + {\\color{red}b}(a - b) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\ta^2 - ab + ab - b^2 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\ta^2 - b^2 \\,& {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n\t}\n$$\n\nNotice that the middle two terms $- ab$ and $ab$ cancel each other out, leaving only $a^2 - b^2.$ \n\nIf the order of multiplication is switched, the formula still remains the same:\n\n$$\n(a - b)(a + b) = a^2 - b^2\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5aaa853f-8b04-5d91-8c05-f30646908746" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8883 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139732 :tutorial/content "If a decimal has an extra zero at the end, we can remove this extra zero without changing the number.\n\nFor example, to find a decimal that's the same as $5.90$, we simply remove the zero at the end:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} 5.9\\cancel{0} = 5.9\n$$\n\nBecause $5.90$ and $5.9$ represent the same decimal number, we say that they are **equivalent decimals**.\n\nWhy does this work? Let's look at the place value chart for $5.90$.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | **.** | $9$ | $0$ |\n\nWe can see that there is a zero in the hundredths column, and there are no smaller place values (for example, there are no thousandths). So, we can remove the hundredths column:\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | **.** | $9$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "f937b221-022f-5fe7-aea9-afaf5e8d7050" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8979 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144004 :tutorial/content "We can also use the standard algorithm for division to divide four and five-digit decimals by whole numbers.\n\nFor instance, to find $50.04 \\div 12$, we start by writing down the problem using long division notation. We keep the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $12$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the dot in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\! \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$12$$\\! \\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!. 1$$7$$12$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$8$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$4$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n50.04 \\div 12 = 4.17\\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5090dd44-0678-5745-a04a-8c6792fcd6a8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2066 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144005 :tutorial/content "To use the standard algorithm, the divisor must be a whole number.\n\nSo, to divide decimals by other decimals such as $0.832 \\div 0.13$, we must find an equivalent division problem by multiplying both the dividend and divisor by an appropriate power of ten.\n\nFirst, we write the division problem $0.832 \\div 0.13$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.832}{0.13}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $100$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.832 \\times 100}{0.13 \\times 100} = \\dfrac{83.2}{13}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $83.2 \\div 13$.\n\nNext, we write down the problem using long division notation, where we keep the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $13$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the dot in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\:\\: \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$13$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $6$$\\!\\!\\!. 4$$13$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,. \\,} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$8$$5$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$2$$\\phantom{0}$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.832 \\div 0.13 = 6.4\\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "059bedca-456e-5669-80dc-b502b1310cb4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2067 :tutorial/title "Dividing Decimals By Decimals Using the Standard Algorithm"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144013 :tutorial/content "We can apply the idea of representing a remainder as a fraction to any problem involving the division of whole numbers. To illustrate, let's write $85 \\div 7$ as a mixed number.\n\nWe start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$\\color{blue}2$$7$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$1$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$\\color{red}1$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we conclude that\n\n$$\n85\\div 7 = {\\color{blue}{12}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($7$). Therefore, expressed as a mixed number, our result is\n\n$$\n85 \\div 7 = {\\color{blue}12} \\, \\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{7}\\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9a978e7c-2910-5ed8-99df-ada0db72fd65" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4850 :tutorial/title "Expressing a Two-Digit by One-Digit Division as a Mixed Number Using Long Division"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144022 :tutorial/content "It's important to interpret quotients and remainders when they arise in real-life problems.\n\nConsider the following situation:\n\n> *Clare must pack $64$ candy bars into boxes. Each box can contain $5$ candy bars. She wishes to create as many fully-filled boxes as possible.*\n\nLet's answer the following questions:\n\n1. How many fully-filled boxes will there be?\n2. How many candy bars will there be in the partially-filled box?\n3. How many boxes will there be in total?\n\nSince the $64$ candy bars are distributed into boxes containing $5$ bars each, we start by dividing $64$ by $5$ using the long division method:\n\n| $1$$2$$5$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$1$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$${4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nThe result of the long division is\n\n$$\n64 \\div 5 = {\\color{blue}{12}}\\,\\text{R}\\,{\\color{red}{4}}\n$$\n\nLet's now answer our questions by interpreting this result:\n\n- The quotient of the division is ${\\color{blue}{12}}$. This means that there will be ${\\color{blue}{12}}$ fully-filled boxes.\n- The remainder of the division is ${\\color{red}{4}}$. This means that there will be ${\\color{red}{4}}$ candy bars in the partially-filled box.\n- Since we have ${\\color{blue}{12}}$ fully-filled boxes and one partially-filled box, there will be ${\\color{blue}{12}}+1 = 13$ boxes in total.\n\nLet's see some more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "9856887a-fcce-56cd-bd91-2760d3b0531b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6711 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144058 :tutorial/content "We can use area models to represent division problems involving three-digit numbers.\n\nLet's consider the area model below.\n\n![](images/7c/7ccfb4c03d006e630f4cf7d5c38e44b06e2647d0d082c30e32250deca4bbc7a9.png)\n\nTo determine the missing length, we consider the area of the yellow rectangle (the one on the left-hand side).\n\nThe area of the yellow rectangle is $320$, and its width is $4$. Hence, the length of this rectangle must be\n$320\\div 4$.\n\nTo carry out this division, we write $320$ as a product with $10$ as one of the factors and then swap the order of multiplication and division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n320 \\div 4 &=  \\\\\n32 \\cdot 10 \\div 4 &=  \\\\\n32 \\div 4 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n(32 \\div 4) \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n8 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n&= 80\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now add this length to the area model.\n\n![](images/ad/addc22099bd0b2ba80e9260f115a991d1299eaf86e4fcebe242656363f389d4c.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "67a6eb45-62d9-5d64-8d17-83e6485ad972" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8517 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144071 :tutorial/content "To solve an expression that contains parentheses, $(\\,)$, we follow the **order of operations with parentheses**. This means that we calculate the operation inside parentheses first, from left to right, before following the standard order of operations.\n\nFor example, let's use the order of operations with parentheses to evaluate $30 \\div (5 \\times 2)$.\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30 \\div (5 \\cdot 2) &=  \\\\\n&= 30 \\div 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30 \\div 10 &=  \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c73a1b14-33f9-5b9c-b640-581391c33369" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8566 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144072 :tutorial/content "Another pair of grouping symbols that are similar to parentheses are square brackets, $[\\:]$.\n\nTo solve an expression that contains both parentheses and brackets, the order of operations with parentheses tells us to:\n\n- Calculate the operations in the parentheses *first*.\n- Calculate the operations in square brackets *second*.\n- Finally, follow the order of operations, as usual.\n\nTo illustrate, let's evaluate the following expression:\n\n$$\n1+ [2 \\times (3 + 4)]\n$$\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + [2 \\cdot (3 + 4)] &=  \\\\\n&= 1 + [2 \\cdot 7]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform the operation inside the square brackets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + [2 \\cdot 7] &=  \\\\\n&= 1 + 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + 14 &=  \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "063858eb-73d6-58c7-9161-ad5726a4de6a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8568 :tutorial/title "Numerical Expressions With Square Brackets"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144073 :tutorial/content "Yet another pair of grouping symbols that are similar to parentheses and square brackets are braces, $\\{\\,\\}$.\n\nFor example, one expression that uses all three grouping symbols is:\n$\\{6 \\div [(3 + 5) \\div 4] \\} + 5$\n\nFor expressions containing parentheses, square brackets, and braces, the order of operations with parentheses tells us to:\n\n- Calculate the operations in the parentheses *first*.\n- Calculate the operations in the square brackets *second*.\n- Calculate the operations in braces *third*.\n- Finally, follow the order of operations, as usual.\n\nSo, to evaluate the expression above, first, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{6 \\div [(3 + 5) \\div 4]} + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= {6 \\div [8 \\div 4]} + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform the operation inside the square brackets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{6 \\div [8 \\div 4]} + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= {6 \\div 2} + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we perform the operation inside the braces:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{6 \\div 2} + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 3 + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c2534ed1-72be-5d1d-a1d4-a07550442b13" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8571 :tutorial/title "Numerical Expressions With Braces"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148662 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n4xy'' + 2y' - y = 0.\n$$\n\nLet's find Frobenius series solutions about the regular singular point $x=0.$\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny=x^\\lambda\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n\n=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^{\\lambda+n}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives $y'$ and $y''{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{'}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{''} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{'}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{y}^{''} & =4x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 2{y}^{'} & =2\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -y & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $n=m-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-y=-\\sum_{m=1}^\\infty a_{m-1}x^{\\lambda+m-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ gives\n\n$$\n-y=-\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^{\\lambda+n-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, isolating the $\\color{blue}n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{y}^{''} & =4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 2{y}^{'} & =2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for ${\\color{blue}n=0},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +2\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x^{\\lambda-1}\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(\\lambda)x^n,\n$$\n\nfor some $b_n(\\lambda),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ 2(2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficients of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary $a_0\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine the possible values of $\\lambda.$ Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (2\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Frobenius series solutions have the forms\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny_2(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^{n+1/2},\n$$\n\nwhere the constants $a_n$ are to be determined separately in each case from the recurrence relation." :tutorial/id #uuid "d4cd6b6a-eb1a-54d1-8279-f6410be29798" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14709 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148797 :tutorial/content "When analyzing data, we often want to know how \"spread out\" the data is. In other words, how far away are the data points from the center?\n\nOne way to represent the spread of the data is to use the **mean absolute deviation (MAD)** of the data. To compute the mean absolute deviation, we\n\n- compute the mean of the data,\n\n- subtract the mean from each number in the data set, find the absolute value of the results, and add all these together, then\n\n- divide the sum by the total number of data points.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's compute the mean absolute deviation of the data set below:\n\n$$\n1, \\: 6, \\: 5\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the mean of the data:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\text{mean}} = \\dfrac{1+6+5}{3} = \\dfrac{12}{3} = {\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the mean from each number in the data set, find the absolute value of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|1-4|+|6-4|+|5-4| & = \\\\ |-3|+|2|+|1| & = \\\\ 3+2+1 & = \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above (${\\color{red}6}$) by the total number of data points ($3$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{3} = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean absolute deviation is $2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9a303783-e636-57c5-bb8e-d9ddd3ec82c8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8944 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153401 :tutorial/content "Given a natural number $n$, **Euler's totient function** $\\phi(n)$ returns the number of positive integers that are less than $n$ and are coprime to $n$. Here, $\\phi$ is the Greek letter phi.\n\nTo illustrate, let's calculate $\\phi(12)$. Since $12$ is a small number, we can first write down all positive integers that are less than $12$, and then remove any numbers that are not coprime to $12$:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} 1, \\: \\bcancel{2}, \\: \\bcancel{3}, \\: \\bcancel{4}, \\: {5}, \\: \\bcancel{6}, \\: {7}, \\: \\bcancel{8}, \\: \\bcancel{9}, \\: \\bcancel{10}, 11\n$$\n\nWe end up with only $4$ positive integers that are less than $12$ and are coprime to $12$. These numbers are $1,5,7$, and $11$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\phi(12) = 4\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "04aa1c1d-e046-5ee0-9791-85ea732e94e3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4514 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153435 :tutorial/content "As we already know, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we must follow the order of operations known as PEMDAS.\n\nThe same rules apply to numerical expressions with negative numbers. For example, let's compute the value of\n\n$$\n\\left( -5 + 7 \\right) \\div (-2) \\times 6.\n$$\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5+7)÷(-2)\\times 6 & = \\\\ 2÷(-2)\\times 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that if both multiplication and division appear in an expression, we complete each operation from left to right (similarly for addition and subtraction).\nSo, we first divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2÷(-2)\\times 6 & = \\\\ -1\\times 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen finally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1\\times 6 & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "26e3e757-ef98-5e88-a3e6-7a753c09e46f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8039 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153468 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following rational number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nWe can find the decimal representation of this rational number by dividing $1$ by $3$ using long division, as follows:\n\n| $\\,\\,0 \\,. 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3$$\\:\\ldots$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$\\,\\ldots$ |\n| --- |\n\nNote that, in this case\n\n- the long division process never ends, and\n- the digit $3$ after the decimal point repeats *forever*.\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13 = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nThis is an example of a **repeating decimal**. A repeating decimal is a decimal where the digits after the decimal point continue forever but in a repeating pattern.\n\nIt's convenient to use the following special notation to indicate decimals that repeat indefinitely.\n\n$$\n0.\\overline{3} = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nSome decimals have two or more digits that repeat indefinitely. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.78^(―) &= 0.78787878… \\\\\n0.675^(―) &= 0.675675675…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSome repeating decimals have non-repeating digits before the repeating part. For example:\n\n$$\n0.333\\overline{67} = 0.333\\,67\\,67\\,67\\,67\\ldots\n$$\n\nThe key takeaway for this section is that every repeating decimal is rational! In other words, every repeating decimal can be expressed as a ratio of two integers.\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.3^(―) &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n0.78^(―) &= \\frac{26}{33} \\\\\n0.33367^(―) &= \\frac{16517}{49500}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn a future lesson, we'll learn how to express a repeating decimal as a fraction. For now, you can verify these results using a calculator." :tutorial/id #uuid "28ff4afd-e8c7-511c-86a4-14d547e6c6d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9072 :tutorial/title "Repeating Decimals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158588 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a binary relation $R$ on $A,$ so $R\\subseteq A^2.$ Then, the following notation is often convenient:\n\n- When $(x,y) \\in R,$ we write $x \\: R \\: y.$ In words, we say \"$x$ is related to $y$ by $R.$\"\n\n- When $(x,y) \\notin R,$ we write $\\require{cancel} x \\cancel{R} y.$ In words, we say \"$x$ is *not* related to $y$ by $R.$\"\n\nFor example, consider the following relation:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\in \\mathbb{Z}^2 \\: : \\: x \\mid y \\big\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe following statements are equivalent:\n\n$$\n(2,10) \\in R\n\\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad\n2 \\: R \\: 10\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n(3,10) \\notin R\n\\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad\n3 \\: {\\cancel{\\!R\\!}} \\: 10\n$$\n\nThe relation $x \\mid y$ is a so-called **standard relation.** With standard relations, we can replace the letter $R$ with the usual symbol describing the relation to retrieve our usual notation:\n\n$$\n(2,10) \\in R \n\\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad \n2 \\: {\\color{red}{R}} \\: 10\n\\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad\n2 \\:{\\color{red}{\\mid}}\\:  10\n$$\n\nFor an ordered pair that's not an element of our relation, we negate the usual symbol.\n\n$$\n(3,10) \\not\\in R\n\\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad \n3 \\: {\\color{red}{\\cancel{\\!R\\!}}} \\: 10\n\\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad\n3 \\:{\\color{red}{\\not\\mid}}\\:  10\n$$\n\nThe same idea applies to other standard relations. For example, consider the set $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\big\\{2,4,6,8,10\\big\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSuppose we define the relations \"is less than\" $(\\lt)$, \"is congruent modulo $2$ to\" $(\\equiv),$ and \"is equal to\" $(=)$ over $A.$ Then, we have the following equivalences.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2,10)\\in {R}_{<} & \\,⇔\\,2\\,{R}_{<}\\,10\\, &  & ⇔\\,2<10 \\\\ (2,4)\\in {R}_{≡} & \\,⇔\\,2\\,{R}_{≡}\\,4\\, &  & ⇔\\,2≡4 \\\\ (6,6)\\in {R}_{=} & \\,⇔\\,6\\,{R}_{=}\\,6\\, &  & ⇔\\,6=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for elements not in the standard relation, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10,2)\\notin {R}_{<} & \\,⇔\\,10\\,\\cancel{\\,{R}_{<}\\,}\\,2\\, &  & ⇔\\,10≮2 \\\\ (1,4)\\notin {R}_{≡} & \\,⇔\\,1\\,\\cancel{\\,{R}_{≡}}\\,\\,4\\, &  & ⇔\\,1≢4 \\\\ (2,4)\\notin {R}_{=} & \\,⇔\\,2\\,\\cancel{\\,{R}_{=}\\,}\\,4\\, &  & ⇔\\,2\\ne 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, not only can relations be thought of as a general way to describe relationships between mathematical objects, but the $a\\,R\\, b$ notation is something you've been using all along!" :tutorial/id #uuid "1841ce90-0dde-5446-89a6-c5fa3ca6ad09" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9063 :tutorial/title "The x R y Notation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158604 :tutorial/content "A number is divisible by $4$ if the *last two digits* are divisible by $4$.\n\nLet's determine whether the following number is divisible by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n124, 424, 6{\\color{blue}{24}}\n$$\n\nThe number formed by the last two digits is ${\\color{blue}{24}}$, and ${\\color{blue}{24}}$ is divisible by $4$. Therefore, $124, 424, 624$ is divisible by $4$.\n\nNow, consider the following number:\n\n$$\n251, 8{\\color{red}{18}}\n$$\n\nThe number formed by the last two digits is ${\\color{red}{18}}$, and ${\\color{red}{18}}$ is *not* divisible by $4$. Therefore, $251, 818$ is *not* divisible by $4$.\n\nWe sometimes need to use long division to determine whether the last two digits are divisible by $4$.\n\nFor example, the number\n$1,7{\\color{blue}{84}}$\nis divisible by $4$ because ${\\color{blue}{84}}$ is divisible by $4$. We can check this using long division.\n\n| $2$$1$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$0$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$4$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nFinally, note that if a number is divisible by $4$, then it is divisible by $2$. Consequently, any number whose last digit is odd is *not* divisible by $4$." :tutorial/id #uuid "91876d6f-1b93-5d6d-adf9-a0af7e58aca7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9438 :tutorial/title "Divisibility by 4"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176965 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to factor the polynomial \n\n$$\n8x^2 + 24x + 16.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it looks tricky because the coefficients are rather large! \n\nHowever, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $8.$ If we first factor out an $8$ from each of the terms, then we can cut the factoring down to something more manageable:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t8x^2 + 24x + 16 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t8(x^2 + 3x + 2)\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the polynomial $x^2 + 3x + 2$ in the parentheses.\n\nThe constant term $(2)$ has a single factor pair, namely\n\n$$\n(1, 2).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the numbers in this factor pair indeed sum to the linear term's coefficient $(1+2=3).$ \n\nSo, we can write the linear term as $3x=x+2x$ and factor as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x+2 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+x+2x+2 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+x)+(2x+2) & = \\\\ x(x+1)+2(x+1) & = \\\\ (x+2)(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}+24x+16 & = \\\\ 8({x}^{2}+3x+2) & = \\\\ 8(x+2)(x+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7336a5d6-0b38-5ba4-b278-aaac6a402ece" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1722 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176971 :tutorial/content "We must be careful when taking the square root of a negative number that's been squared.\n\nFor example, $\\sqrt{(-9)^2}$ is *not* equal to $-9!$ We can write this using a **not equal to** symbol $\\neq$ as\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(-9)^2}  \\neq -9.\n$$\n\nThis is because the square root of a number can *only* be positive or zero, never negative.\nLet's work it out the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(-9)\\cdot (-9)} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{81} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{9}^{2}} & = \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(-9)^2}=\\sqrt{9^2} = 9.\n$$\n\nFor any positive perfect square, we always have exactly two numbers whose squares equal that number. Here, we have\n\n$$\n9^2=81\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad (-9)^2=81.\n$$\n\nBut *by definition* the square root is always *positive* (or zero) and *cannot* be negative. So here, $\\sqrt{81}=+9,$ and for any non-negative $\\color{blue}\\square$ we get\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\color{blue}\\square} \\geq 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "27f63bb3-bf90-584e-b96c-d999c482f4e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3094 :tutorial/title "The Square Root of a Negative Number Squared"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177090 :tutorial/content "We can use place value strategies to multiply any whole number by $10$.\n\nFor example, let's use our understanding of place value to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n4 \\times 10\n$$\n\nRemember that $4 \\times 10$ means \"$4$ groups of $10$.\" Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n\\underbrace{10 + 10 + 10 + 10}_{4\\,\\text{times}} &=  \\\\\n4tens + 0ones &=  \\\\\n&= 40 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe same computation can be explained visually as follows:\n\n![](images/40/4090d12e2dacc197273836a0a6364dcfbdeed6123bc31c6fe0c42907bb0de473.png)\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n4\\times 10 = 40\n$$\n\nWe can use the same strategy to multiply two-digit numbers by $10$, too. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "cd87448c-bc5f-500c-b245-1c7f00358618" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8482 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180669 :tutorial/content "Remember that in order to add fractions with different denominators, like\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 4 + \\dfrac 1 8\n$$\n\nwe need to put the fractions over a **common denominator**, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{8} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2}{8} + \\frac{1}{8} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt works the same in algebra! To add two fractions, we need to put each fraction over a common denominator. Then, we add the numerators." :tutorial/id #uuid "f5b564ba-0934-5110-87ce-f04259821232" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3377 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180733 :tutorial/content "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nHowever, if the domain of the variable is restricted, some expressions might allow further simplification. \n\nFor example, suppose we know that $a>0.$ Let's use this information to fully simplify $\\sqrt{36a^2}.$\n\nWe can start by simplifying the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{36{a}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(6a)^{2}} & = \\\\ |6a| & = \\\\ |6|\\cdot |a| \\\\ 6|a| & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $a>0,$ and we know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have $|a|=a.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n6|a| = 6a .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "414812ea-abcc-59df-98fa-8a79ae36a1a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5905 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180736 :tutorial/content "Let's see what changes if we consider square roots of perfect squares when some variables are negative.\n\nFor example, suppose that $t \\lt 0.$ Let's use this to simplify the expression $\\sqrt{t^2}.$\n\nSimplifying this expression in the usual way, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sqrt{t^2}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\\sqrt{(t)^2}&=\\[5pt]\n\t|t|.&\n\t}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $t \\lt 0.$ To take the absolute value of a negative number, we need to flip the sign. This gives $|t| = -t.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{t^2}} = {\\color{red}-t}.\n$$\n\nYou can convince yourself that this is correct by plugging a few values of $t$ into the above equation. Provided that $t$ is negative, this expression will always give a true statement.\n\nFor example, suppose that $t = -2.$ Substituting into the left-hand side of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & = \\\\ 2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=-2$ into the right-hand side of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-t & = \\\\ -(-2) & = \\\\ 2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides give the same result, the statement is true." :tutorial/id #uuid "1f0207ef-9d6a-584b-8d44-a92c732cce4b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5928 :tutorial/title "The Square Root of a Perfect Square With Negative Variables"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180739 :tutorial/content "When dealing with sums or differences of square roots, we can combine any terms with the same algebraic expression under the square root symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}.$ This is similar to collecting like terms in a linear expression.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n5\\sqrt{x} - 3\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{x}-3\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ (5-3)\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe *cannot* combine terms when the expressions under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n5\\sqrt{x} - 3\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt x$ and $\\sqrt y.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "deda60a8-4b35-50ac-a71c-d3f884aa8e1d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5971 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180747 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a few powers of $2$ and their corresponding binary notations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{0} &= (1)_{2} \\\\\n2^{1} &= (10)_{2} \\\\\n2^{2} &= (100)_{2} \\\\\n2^{3} &= (1000)_{2} \\\\\n&= ⋮ \\\\\n2^{n} &= (1\\underbrace{00…0}_{{n}\\text{ times}})_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in base $2$, the powers of $2$ look similar to powers of $10$ in base $10$. It turns out that they also play a similar role in multiplication and division.\n\n- To multiply a binary integer by a power of $2$, we append the corresponding number of zeros to the right of the number. For example,\n$(101)_2 \\cdot (1{\\underbrace{\\color{blue}000}_{ { {3} \\textrm{ zeros}}}})_2 = (101{\\underbrace{\\color{blue}000}_{ { {3} \\textrm{ zeros}}}})_2 .$\n- To divide a binary integer by a power of $2$, we erase the corresponding number of zeros on the right of the number. For example,\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} (111\\cancel{{\\color{blue}00}})_2 \\div (1\\cancel{{\\color{blue}00}})_2 = (111)_2\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "afa59cc2-1dc3-58e1-8774-43d4336bac81" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6002 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180750 :tutorial/content "We can simplify cube root expressions in the same way as with square roots.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt[3]x - 2\\sqrt[3] x\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt[3]{x}-2\\sqrt[3]{x} & = \\\\ (4-2)\\sqrt[3]{x} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt[3]{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs with square roots, we *cannot* combine terms when the expressions under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x-1} + \\sqrt[3]{x+1}\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt[3]{x-1}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{x+1}.$\n\nFinally, it is not possible to combine square root and cube root terms, even if the numbers under the radical symbol are the same! For example, the expression\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt x + \\sqrt[3] x\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt x$ and $\\sqrt[3] x.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a4a25921-e2bd-5bf7-99ed-3a8dd852fb70" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6013 :tutorial/title "Simplifying Expressions with Cube Roots"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185326 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a sample of size $n,$ the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2.\n$$\n\nWe will now prove that $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2,$ that is,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\left[S^2\\right] = \\sigma^2.\n$$\n\nA major step towards this goal is to find the expected value of the sum of squares term:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E \\left[ \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 \\right] \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFirst, consider the following sum of squares:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this term by adding and subtracting the sample mean $\\overline X,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n \\left((X_i - \\overline X) + (\\overline X -  \\mu)\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSquaring and applying the laws of summations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{(({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})+(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu ))}^{2} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+2({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )+(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2}) & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+2(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+n(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have used the fact that $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i - \\overline X) = 0$ and that $(\\overline X - \\mu)$ does not depend on $i$. Thus, we have that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2 =  \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 + n(\\overline X-  \\mu)^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 = \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2 -  n(\\overline X-  \\mu)^2  . \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast\\ast)\n$$\n\nNotice that the left-hand side is the summation term $(\\ast)$ we wish to find the expectation of.\n\nNow, we make use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}] & ={\\sigma }^{2} \\\\ \\text{E}[(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2}] & =\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinding the expectation of $(\\ast\\ast),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}] & =\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}]-n\\text{E}[(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2}] \\\\  & =n{\\sigma }^{2}-n\\cdot \\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n} \\\\  & =n{\\sigma }^{2}-{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =(n-1){\\sigma }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}]=\\frac{(n-1){\\sigma }^{2}}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis tells us that the statistic\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2\n$$\n\nis a *biased* estimate of $\\sigma^2.$ Therefore, to form an unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2,$ we must divide our summation term by $n-1,$ not $n.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{S}^{2}] & =\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}\\cdot (n-1){\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "bc43298c-5d5c-5750-8ac9-a9518e92f1bc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6032 :tutorial/title "Proof That the Sample Variance Is an Unbiased Estimator"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185346 :tutorial/content "The **product rule for radicals** states that the square root of a product equals the product of the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sqrt{a} \\cdot \\sqrt{b} = \\sqrt{ab}\n\t}\n$$\n\nWe can use the product rule to combine radical expressions. \n\nFor example, let's suppose we want to combine the radicals in the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5x} \\cdot \\sqrt{6z}\n$$\n\nTo combine the radicals, we apply the product rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5x}\\cdot \\sqrt{6z} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{5x\\cdot 6z} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{30xz} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1c9d8f0d-315b-5c83-a8e6-17638c1c3d24" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6200 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185350 :tutorial/content "We can compute trigonometric ratios using the sum and difference formulas for cosine together with the Pythagorean identity.\n\nLet's suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac{1}{3}, \\qquad\n\\sin v = \\dfrac {3}{5}, \\qquad\n\\cos v = -\\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the value of $\\cos\\left(u - v\\right)$ given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant?\n\nFirst, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\cos(u - v) \n\t\t\t&= \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\left( -\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\right) \\left(-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right) +  \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right) \\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right) \\[5pt]\t\t\t\n \t\t\t&= \\dfrac{8\\sqrt{2}}{15} + \\dfrac{3}{15} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{8\\sqrt{2}+3}{15}\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "75e177a6-4974-5699-b211-c562c0b8bafe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6209 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185361 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to add numbers in scientific notation with different powers of ten.\n\nFor example, let's compute the following sum:\n\n$$\n(4\\times 10^{\\color{blue}{5}}) + (2 \\times 10^{\\color{red}{4}})\n$$\n\nNotice that the two numbers in our sum have different powers of ten (${\\color{blue}{5}}$ and ${\\color{red}{4}},$ respectively).\n\nTo add these two numbers, we use the following steps:\n\n- **Step 1**: Identify the smaller number in the sum.\n\n- **Step 2**: Rewrite the smaller number to have the same power of ten as the larger number.\n\n- **Step 3**: Add the two numbers as usual.\n\nIn our sum, the *second* number $(2\\times 10^{\\color{red}{4}})$ is the smaller of the two because it has the smallest power of ten.\n\nSo, we rewrite this number so that its power of ten is ${\\color{blue}{5}},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\times {10}^{4} & = \\\\ 2\\times {10}^{5-1} & = \\\\ 2\\times {10}^{-1+5} & = \\\\ 2\\times {10}^{-1}\\times {10}^{5} & = \\\\ 0.2\\,\\times {10}^{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, carrying out the addition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4\\times {10}^{5})+(2\\times {10}^{4}) & = \\\\ (4\\times {10}^{5})+(0.2\\times {10}^{5}) & = \\\\ (4+0.2)\\times {10}^{5} & = \\\\ 4.2\\times {10}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a5fd6814-3ee6-5f3c-aed4-1d64fb180b9f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6657 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185368 :tutorial/content "The **distributive law** states that multiplying a group of terms by a number is the same as multiplying each term separately by that same number and adding the products.\n\nThe distributive law works no matter how many terms are inside the parentheses.\n\nFor example, let's take a look at the following expression:\n\n$$\n4(a+2b+3)\n$$\n\nWe can expand this expression using the distributive law:\n\n![](images/07/07b3158ef747d1c5223ed2fa2b8c7d7df11e8d55274279fa69720543fc9d1137.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(a + 2b + 3) &=  \\\\\n4 \\cdot a + 4 \\cdot 2b + 4 \\cdot 3 &=  \\\\\n(4a) + (8b) + (12) &=  \\\\\n&= 4a + 8b + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distributive law also applies when the multiplication occurs on the right of the parentheses. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "9ab82c8a-c00e-5b66-a2d9-137ff2dd636a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8057 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185369 :tutorial/content "The distributive law also works when there is subtraction inside the parentheses.\n\nFor example, we can expand the expression $2(x+2y-3)$ as follows:\n\n![](images/67/675d42051db7ea414b842c48528a3da3fb03e4bd7693d460f02f1cf1a565e452.png)\n\nNotice that we keep plus signs ($+$) between the three terms. This helps us to avoid mistakes when multiplying with negative numbers.\n\nNow, evaluating our expression, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(x + 2y - 3) &=  \\\\\n2 \\cdot x + 2 \\cdot 2y + 2 \\cdot (-3) &=  \\\\\n(2x) + (4y) + (-6) &=  \\\\\n&= 2x + 4y - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5ba5c662-9618-5304-855b-9a0fe65a0d70" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8058 :tutorial/title "Dealing With Minus Signs Inside the Parentheses"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189437 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n12\\div 4 \\times 7\n$$\n\nNotice that this expression contains multiplication *and* division. So how do we compute its value?\n\nTo help us, we have the following rule:\n\n> *When an expression contains only multiplication and division, we carry out the operations from left to right*.\n\nSo, we must first carry out the division $(12\\div 4)$. We can emphasize this using parentheses $(\\phantom{0}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\big(12\\div 4\\big) \\times 7\n$$\n\nHere, the parentheses tell us which part of the expression to do first.\n\nNow, we evaluate our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(12 \\div 4) \\cdot 7 &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 7 &=  \\\\\n&= 21\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "714cbc07-ff21-5cfe-be50-8078a2f54b22" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8066 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189438 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n10{\\color{red}{\\,\\div\\, 6\\,}} {\\color{blue}{\\,\\times\\, 3}}\n$$\n\nWe carry out the operations from left to right. However, the division problem is difficult because $10$ cannot be divided evenly into groups of $6$. So, what do we do?\n\nTo help us, we have the following rule:\n\n> *When the only operations are multiplication and division, we can swap the order in which the operations are carried out.*\n\nTherefore, we can swap the order of the multiplication and division as follows:\n\n$$\n10{\\color{blue}{\\,\\times\\, 3}}{\\color{red}{\\,\\div\\, 6\\,}}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate our expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 \\cdot 3 \\div 6 &=  \\\\\n(10 \\cdot 3) \\div 6 &=  \\\\\n30 \\div 6 &=  \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c1655aa9-9523-51dd-ad4a-2086a29c245b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8067 :tutorial/title "Swapping Multiplication and Division"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189440 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n6x-(3+2x)\n$$\n\nIn this example, we're subtracting the entire group of terms in parentheses from $6x.$ In such cases, we distribute the negative sign to each term in the parentheses *first* and then simplify by collecting like terms.\n\nRemoving the parentheses by distributing the negative sign, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-(3+2x) & = \\\\ 6x-3-2x & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-3-2x & = \\\\ (6x-2x)-3 & = \\\\ 4x-3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's take a look at another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "e0305f7d-c5ac-5566-af24-891e025ad938" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8189 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189441 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, to subtract a group of terms, we have to apply the distributive law.\n\nFor example, let's simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n7-4(y+2)\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n7 - \\Big[4(y+2)\\Big]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n7 - \\Big[4y+8\\Big].\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as before. Removing the brackets by distributing the negative sign gives\n\n$$\n7 - 4y - 8.\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-4y-8 & = \\\\ (7-8)-4y & = \\\\ -1-4y & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7ded6b18-fb82-5e0c-999c-e8bede8f99c8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8190 :tutorial/title "Subtracting a Group of Terms Using the Distributive Law"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189466 :tutorial/content "Using what we've learned, we can now add fractions with denominators $10$ and $100$.\n\nFor example, let's find the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{10} + \\dfrac{3}{100}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The denominators of our two fractions are *not* the same. Therefore, we *cannot* add these fractions straight off the bat.\n- However, we *can* add these fractions if we convert the first fraction to an equivalent decimal fraction with a denominator of $100$ before adding.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{10}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $100$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{10} = \\dfrac{5 \\times 10}{10 \\times 10} = \\dfrac{50}{100}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{10} + \\frac{3}{100} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{50}{100} + \\frac{3}{100} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{50 + 3}{100} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{53}{100}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{10} + \\dfrac{3}{100} = \\dfrac{53}{100}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f8c16e46-c8fc-5ff8-9358-1ce23b7ac27e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8438 :tutorial/title "Adding Fractions With Denominators of Ten and One Hundred"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189468 :tutorial/content "Let's convert the following mixed number to an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2}\\,\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nEvery mixed number can be written as the sum of its whole number and fractional parts. So, our mixed number is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{3}}}\n$$\n\nNext, we write the whole number part $({\\color{red}{2}})$ as a fraction with $1$ in the denominator:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac21}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this over the same denominator as the fractional part of our mixed number. So, in this case, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\dfrac21} = \\dfrac{{\\color{red}2} \\times 3}{{\\color{red}{1}} \\times 3} = {\\color{red}{\\dfrac{6}{3}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add our fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\frac{1}{3} &=  \\\\\n2 + \\frac{1}{3} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{6}{3} + \\frac{1}{3} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{6 + 1}{3} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nexpressed as a mixed number is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac73\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ff763334-d44a-5312-87d1-09be5bad829b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8447 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189478 :tutorial/content "We can use place value strategies to divide some large numbers quickly and efficiently.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's discuss how to use place value strategies to solve the following division problem:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{20}} \\div 2\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the number we're dividing $({\\color{red}{20}})$ in expanded form:\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 10 \\div 2\n$$\n\nThen, we swap the order of the multiplication and division and evaluate the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 \\cdot 10 \\div 2 &=  \\\\\n2 \\div 2 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n(2 \\div 2) \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n1 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n20 \\div 2 = 10\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "58bd3215-6352-5746-bc30-118e9e65d636" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8471 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189479 :tutorial/content "To multiply a whole number by $10$, we attach a zero to the end of the number.\n\nFor example, to find the value of $52\\times 10$, we attach an extra zero to the end of $52{:}$\n\n$$\n52\\times 10 = 52{\\color{blue}{0}}\n$$\n\nWe know that multiplication and division are opposite operations. So, to *divide* a number by ten, we *remove* a zero from the end of the number. For example,\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} 520 \\div 10 = 52\\!\\cancel{0} = 52\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** This method only works for *whole numbers* that end in at least one zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "13618399-0779-58bd-b39d-ca3075c37e44" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8477 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189480 :tutorial/content "We can use a similar method to divide whole numbers by $100$ and $1,000$.\n\nTo divide a number by one hundred, we remove *two* zeros from the end of the number. For example,\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} 6,500 \\div 100 = 6,5\\!\\cancel{00} = 65\n$$\n\nThis method only works for whole numbers that end in at least *two* zeros.\n\nSimilarly, to divide a whole number by one thousand, we remove *three* zeros from the end of the number. For example,\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} 32,000 \\div 1,000 = 32,\\!\\!\\cancel{000} = 32\n$$\n\nThis method only works for whole numbers that end in at least *three* zeros." :tutorial/id #uuid "727e1dff-7925-5b5d-a57d-34c94a66b7ac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8479 :tutorial/title "Dividing Whole Numbers by One Hundred and One Thousand"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193842 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll extend our understanding of angles to real-world and mathematical problems.\n\nFor example, let's find the angle formed by the minute and hour hands of a clock at $5$:$00$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9d/9daac7fc80bbb9e032e7e0f459b66b9b1c08a083ff4ab6bf67882c737a3eee38.png)\n\nIf we connect the center of the clock face and the hour marks, we get $12$ identical angles. And, since a full angle has a measure of $360^\\circ$, each of these angles has a measure of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{12}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the value of this fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{360}{12} &=  \\\\\n360 \\div 12 &=  \\\\\n36 \\cdot 10 \\div 12 &=  \\\\\n(36 \\div 12) \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{12} = 30^\\circ\n$$\n\nThe angle formed by the minute and hour hands of a clock at $5$:$00$ contains $5$ such angles, so its measure is\n\n$$\n5 \\times 30^\\circ = 150^\\circ\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a40f2335-e116-54c1-b8ce-9f6a4f04cd97" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6621 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193843 :tutorial/content "![](images/04/0420b22be0c7c084c39d99e7308e83bb05feb44c1e7ae29222f53e98b0baa200.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\angle{AOB}$ shown above represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nof a full turn. How can we find the measure of this angle?\n\nThe angle $\\angle AOB$ represents one-tenth of a full turn. Since a full turn has a measure of $360^\\circ$, the measure of $\\angle AOB$ is one-tenth of $360^\\circ$.\n\nTherefore, we can express the degree measure of $\\angle{AOB}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{10} \\cdot 360^{∘} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1 \\cdot 360^{∘}}{10} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{360^{∘}}{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's common to write the fraction in parentheses and place the degree symbol outside, as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{360}{10})^{∘}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify this fraction by expressing it as a division.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{360}{10}} & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle 360 \\div  10 &  \\\\ \\displaystyle 36 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{0} &  \\\\ \\displaystyle 36 & \\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the measure of $\\angle AOB$ is $36^\\circ$." :tutorial/id #uuid "985d6c0a-e42d-5fad-99fa-c411e32b109d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6623 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193849 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, when adding two numbers in scientific notation, we get a number that's not in scientific notation. In such cases, we need to rewrite the final result. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the following sum:\n\n$$\n\\left(9 \\times 10^{7}\\right) + \\left(9.3 \\times 10^{8}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe first number in the sum is the smaller of the two, so we rewrite it to have the same power of $10$ as the second number, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\times {10}^{7} & = \\\\ 9\\times {10}^{8-1} & = \\\\ 9\\times {10}^{-1+8} & = \\\\ 9\\times {10}^{-1}\\times {10}^{8} \\\\ 0.9\\times {10}^{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can carry out the addition as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9\\times {10}^{7})+(9.3\\times {10}^{8}) &= (0.9\\times {10}^{8})+(9.3\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\ &= (0.9+9.3)\\times {10}^{8} \\\\ &= 10.2\\times {10}^{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above number is not in scientific notation because $10.2 \\gt      10.$ So, we need to write $10.2$ in scientific notation, as follows:\n\n$$\n10.2 = 1.02\\times 10^{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10.2\\times {10}^{8} &= (1.02\\times {10}^{1})\\times {10}^{8} \\\\ &= 1.02\\times ({10}^{1}\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\ &= 1.02\\times {10}^{1+8} \\\\ &= 1.02\\times {10}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our final answer is $1.02 \\times 10^{9}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4c243eec-547f-5511-92b1-6fae1c7e37a0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6667 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193851 :tutorial/content "The method of subtracting numbers in scientific notation with different powers of ten is similar to adding them. We follow the usual three steps:\n\n- **Step 1**: Identify the smaller number.\n\n- **Step 2**: Rewrite the smaller number to have the same power of ten as the larger number.\n\n- **Step 3**: Subtract the two numbers as usual.\n\nFor instance, let's compute the following difference:\n\n$$\n\\left(9 \\times 10^{7} \\right) - \\left(8 \\times 10^{6} \\right)\n$$\n\nThe second number is the smaller of the two, so we rewrite it to have the same power of $10$ as the first number, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\times {10}^{6} &= 8\\times {10}^{7-1} \\\\ &= 8\\times {10}^{-1+7} \\\\ &= 8\\times {10}^{-1}\\times {10}^{7} \\\\ &= 0.8\\times {10}^{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can carry out the subtraction as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9\\times {10}^{7})-(8\\times {10}^{6}) &= (9\\times {10}^{7})-(0.8\\times {10}^{7}) \\\\ &= (9-0.8)\\times {10}^{7} \\\\ &= 8.2\\times {10}^{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0501ed36-b116-5da5-a292-9d46f8399624" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6685 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193856 :tutorial/content "When we **round a number to the nearest whole number**, we find the closest whole number to our number.\n\nRounding a number to the nearest whole number is often useful when dividing two whole numbers with remainders.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's round the result of the following division problem to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n79 \\div 6\n$$\n\nWe start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $1$$3$$6$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$9$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$8$$1$ |\n| --- |\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n79 \\div 6 = 13 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 1\n$$\n\nWriting this result as a mixed number, we obtain\n\n$$\n79 \\div 6 = 13 \\, {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{6}}\n$$\n\nWe need to round this result *to the nearest whole number*. So, we need to decide whether to round down or round up.\n\nNote that,\n\n- rounding the answer *down* gives $13$, while\n- rounding the answer *up* gives $14$.\n\nTo decide whether to round down or up, we need to decide which of these two options $(13$ or $14)$ is closest to\n\n$$\n13 \\, {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{6}}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we compare the fractional part of our answer to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\dfrac12}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{6}} < {\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nthis tells us that\n\n$$\n13 \\, {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{6}}\n$$\n\nis *closer* to $13$ than to $14$.\n\n![](images/23/23a39533f5076d46a867cda7e2ac72b7e15d5bc6b5c907809813b3ff75fe2471.png)\n\nTherefore, we need to round *down*:\n\n$$\n13 \\, {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{6}} \\to 13\n$$\n\nTherefore, $79 \\div 6$ rounded to the nearest whole number is $13$." :tutorial/id #uuid "3b9a3d04-5744-577c-ac25-7f4469b24b71" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6710 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193911 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n5 + 3 \\times 7\n$$\n\nNotice that this expression contains addition *and* multiplication. So how do we compute its value?\n\nTo help us, we have the following rule:\n\n> *When an expression contains only multiplication and addition (or subtraction), the multiplication should always be carried out first*.\n\nSo, we must first carry out the multiplication $(3\\times 7)$. We can emphasize this using parentheses $(\\phantom{0}){:}$\n\n$$\n5 + \\big({\\color{blue}3 \\times 7}\\big)\n$$\n\nHere, the parentheses tell us which part of the expression to do first.\n\nNow, we evaluate our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 + (3 \\cdot 7) &=  \\\\\n5 + 21 &=  \\\\\n&= 26\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If we carried out the operations in a different order, we'd obtain an *incorrect answer!*\n\nFor example, if we perform the addition first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5 + 3) \\cdot 7 &=  \\\\\n8 \\cdot 7 &=  \\\\\n&= 56 \\mid \\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *incorrect!*" :tutorial/id #uuid "62024a7a-6024-5f90-806d-813a28148c9e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8043 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193920 :tutorial/content "We can also borrow from other place values.\n\nFor example, let's consider the equation below.\n\n$$\n{ {2} \\textrm{ hundreds}} = { {1} \\textrm{ hundred}} + { {\\bbox[2pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A A}}} \\textrm{ tens}}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we want to borrow $1$ from the *hundreds* place and convert it to *tens*.\n\nFirst, notice that we can write \"$2$ hundreds\" as follows:\n\n$$\n[math]2[/math] hundreds = [math]\\color{red}1[/math] hundred + [math]\\color{blue}1[/math] hundred\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $1$ hundred into $10$ tens:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[math]\\color{red}1[/math] hundred + [math]\\color{blue}1[/math] hundred &=  \\\\\n&= [math]\\color{red}1[/math] hundred + [math]\\color{blue}10[/math] tens\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{ {2} \\textrm{ hundreds}} \n= \n{ {1} \\textrm{ hundred}} + { {\\bbox[2pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{10}} \\textrm{ tens}} .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1cf489fc-3a70-5323-bdc5-b69a8644fcc3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8396 :tutorial/title "Borrowing From Hundreds"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199493 :tutorial/content "**Measuring** means finding a number that describes the size of something. In particular, we determine the duration between two events by measuring the **time** between those events. We use **time units** for this purpose.\n\nThe most popular measurement system within the scientific community is the **metric system**. Within the metric system, the most common units of measurement are called **base units**.\n\nThe base unit for time is the **second**, which we denote using the symbol \"$\\textrm{s}.$\" We measure the time between two events using clocks like the ones shown below.\n\n![](images/93/93026f119d483006ac3e2a58c4b31d68f6a25618afc0b998da74f4914ca1c1c7.png)\n\nWe use **milliseconds** to measure smaller time intervals. The symbol for milliseconds is \"$\\textrm{ms}.$\" The prefix $\\textrm m$ stands for **milli,** which means $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}.$ Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{ms}$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\textrm s.$\n\nNotice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{ms}$ to itself $1\\,000$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{s}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{1\\,\\text{ms}+1\\,\\text{ms}+⋯+1\\,\\text{ms}}^{1\\,000\\text{ times}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s}+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s}+⋯+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s} & = \\\\ 1\\,000\\times \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000\\times 1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{s} & = \\\\ 1\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{ms}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{s},$ as stated in the diagram.\n\nOne millisecond is a very short time interval. To put this into context, a single blink of a human eye is around $200$ milliseconds on average." :tutorial/id #uuid "3f956ba1-1775-5b26-babc-1e86bba4a909" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7790 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199494 :tutorial/content "**Measuring** means finding a number that describes the size of something. The most popular measurement system within the scientific community is the **metric system**. \n\nWe determine how much space an object occupies by measuring its **volume**. We use **volume units** to specify an object's volume. \n\nA common metric unit of volume is the **liter,** which we denote using the symbol \"$\\textrm{L}$.\" We can use **measuring jugs** like the ones below to measure the volume of a liquid.\n\n![](images/fa/fa15e2d9b446412876732435aa06b8117fbb4903ab179e4cc64c50da4868fee7.png)\n\nWe use **milliliters** to measure smaller volumes. The symbol for milliliters is \"$\\textrm{mL}.$\" The prefix $\\textrm m$ stands for **milli,** which means $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}.$ Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{mL}$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\textrm L.$\n\nNotice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{mL}$ to itself $1\\,000$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{L}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{1\\,\\text{mL}+1\\,\\text{mL}+⋯+1\\,\\text{mL}}^{1\\,000\\text{ times}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L}+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L}+⋯+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L} & = \\\\ 1\\,000\\times \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000\\times 1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{L} & = \\\\ 1\\,\\text{L}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{L},$ as stated in the diagram." :tutorial/id #uuid "134c077c-df22-5aea-bfa9-f7c4716a73cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7791 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199514 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the table that relates metric units of mass.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\n**Important**: Symbols in the metric system follow a consistent set of **prefix rules**, which we explain below. If you find these difficult, *do not worry*! For now, you just need to remember the conversions shown in the table above. \n\nThe prefix rules are as follows:\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm k$\" stands for **kilo**, which means $1\\,000$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}.$\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm m$\" stands for **milli**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{1,000}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{mg}=\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\textrm{g}.$ \n\nNotice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{mg}$ to itself $1\\,000$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{g}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{1\\,\\text{mg}+1\\,\\text{mg}+⋯+1\\,\\text{mg}}^{1\\,000\\text{ times}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g}+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g}+⋯+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ 1\\,000\\times \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000\\times 1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ 1\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{g},$ as stated in the table." :tutorial/id #uuid "4c656aea-de27-5e2c-9a0f-d9a229d4e0c5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7944 :tutorial/title "The Prefix System for Metric Units"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205235 :tutorial/content "Since relations are simply sets of ordered pairs, all the operations defined on sets, such as complement, union, and intersection, apply to relations.\n\nThe **complement** of a relation $R$ defined over the sets $A$ and $B$ is the subset of the Cartesian product $A \\times B$ containing all pairs that do not belong to $R{:}$\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\overline{R} = \\big\\{(x,y) \\in A \\times B \\::\\: x \\cancel{R} y \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the relation\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(1, 1), (1, 3), (2, 1), (2, 2), (3,3) \\big\\}\n$$\n\ndefined on\n\n$$\nA = \\{1,2,3 \\}\n$$\n\nLet's write out the elements of $A^2$, highlighting the pairs that belong to $R{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(1, 1) & (1, 2) & (1, 3) \\\\ (2, 1) & (2, 2) & (2, 3) \\\\ (3, 1) & (3, 2) & (3, 3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAll the remaining pairs belong to the complement of $R{:}$\n\n$$\n\\overline{R} = \\big\\{(1,2), (2,3), (3,1), (3,2) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe intersection and union are even more straightforward.\n\nThe **intersection** of the relations $R$ and $S$, each defined over the sets $A$ and $B$, is a subset of the Cartesian product $A \\times B$ that consists of all the pairs that belong to $R$ *and* $S{:}$\n\n$$\nR \\cap S = \\big\\{(x,y) \\in A \\times B \\,:\\, x\\:R\\:y \\:\\:\\textrm{and}\\:\\: x\\:S\\:y \\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the intersection contains only the pairs that belong to both relations.\n\nThe **union** of the relations $R$ and $S$, each defined over the sets $A$ and $B$, is a subset of the Cartesian product $A \\times B$ that consists of all the pairs that belong to $R$ *or* $S{:}$\n\n$$\nR \\cup S = \\big\\{(x,y) \\in A \\times B \\,:\\, x\\:R\\:y \\:\\:\\textrm{or}\\:\\: x\\:S\\:y \\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the union contains all the pairs that belong to one of the relations.\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "ad7d8a33-045c-50b9-90be-a0fdeb345480" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9057 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205236 :tutorial/content "Recall that a binary relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A = A^2{:}$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A^2\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we have the following notations:\n\n- $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $(x,y) \\in R$\n\n- $\\require{cancel}x \\: \\cancel{\\!R\\!} \\: y$ if and only if $(x,y) \\notin R$\n\nRelations can be defined over infinite sets. For example, consider the relation $R$ on $\\mathbb{Z}$ defined by\n\n$$\nx \\: R \\: y \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad x \\geq y.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $x \\geq y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $\\geq$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\!R\\!}$ and $\\not\\geq$ (or, equivalently, $\\cancel{\\!R\\!}$ and $\\lt$) are also interchangeable.\n\nFor example:\n\n- $2 \\: R \\: 0$ since $2 \\geq 0$\n\n- $\\require{cancel} 1 \\: \\cancel{\\!R\\!} \\: \\pi$ since $1 \\not\\geq \\pi$\n\n- $0 \\: R \\: 0$ since $0 \\geq 0$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{Z}^2$ such that $x \\geq y,$ we can write out $R$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\in \\mathbb{Z}^2 \\: : \\: x \\geq y \\big\\}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f63d42cd-e795-5986-8ce3-11dc8e48fcaa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9060 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205291 :tutorial/content "When factoring a trinomial, we should always begin by factoring out any constant factors. Then, we factor whatever quadratic remains using the techniques we've studied. \n\nFor example, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n6y^2+21y+9\n$$\n\nThe first thing to notice is that all the terms have a common factor of $3.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n3(2y^2 + 7y + 3)\n$$\n\nSo, our goal now is to factor the trinomial in parentheses:\n\n$$\n2y^2 + 7y + 3\n$$\n\nTo do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times 3 = 6$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nAlso, since all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the *positive* factor pairs of $6.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $6$ are\n\n$$\n(1,6),\\quad (2,3).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,6)$ add to $7{:}$ \n\n$$\n1+6 =7  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $y+6y,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}+7y+3= \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+y+6y+3 & = \\\\ (2{y}^{2}+y)+(6y+3) & = \\\\ y(2y+1)+3(2y+1) & = \\\\ (y+3)(2y+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{2}+21y+9 & = \\\\ 3(2{y}^{2}+7y+3) & = \\\\ 3(y+3)(2y+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nWhenever the coefficient of the quadratic term is negative, we should always factor this out first. That way, we're left with the task of factoring a trinomial containing a positive quadratic coefficient. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "35b6c679-c9c6-5b44-8de5-21106bc70674" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9456 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186214235 :tutorial/content "Rationalizing the denominator works similarly for algebraic expressions as it does for numerical ones. When the denominator contains two terms and includes radicals, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the *conjugate* of the denominator to eliminate the radical.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{1+\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the conjugate of the denominator $1+\\sqrt{x},$ all we have to do is flip the sign between the two terms. So, the conjugate of the denominator is \n\n$$\n1\\,{\\color{blue}{-}}\\,\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $1-\\sqrt{x}$ and simplify:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1+\\sqrt{x}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{1-\\sqrt{x}}{1-\\sqrt{x}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{x}}{(1+\\sqrt{x})(1-\\sqrt{x})} & = \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{x}}{{1}^{2}-(\\sqrt{x})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{x}}{1-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8dde46d5-669e-5c3a-b471-7541b1824862" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13464 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186214282 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to calculate conditional probabilities from frequency tables. The key is to recognize that we will usually be comparing two counts:\n\n- the number of outcomes that satisfy both the *event* $(A)$ and the *condition* $(B)$, and\n- the number of outcomes that satisfy the condition alone.\n\nRecall that in symbols, the conditional probability is given by\n\n$$\nP(A \\mid B) = \\dfrac{P(A \\cap B)}{P(B)} = \\dfrac{\\#(A \\cap B)}{\\#(B)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\#$ denotes the function that returns the number of elements in the corresponding set.\n\nFor example, the table below shows the distribution of participants’ workout durations.\n\n| Workout Duration (minutes) | Number of Participants |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0-19$ | $5$ |\n| $20-39$ | $8$ |\n| $40-59$ | $12$ |\n| $60-79$ | $9$ |\n| $80-100$ | $4$ |\n\nIf a participant who exercised at least $40$ minutes is selected at random, what is the probability that the participant exercised at least $60$ minutes?\n\nTo determine the desired probability, we must find the fraction of participants who exercised at least $60$ minutes among those who exercised at least $40$ minutes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Exercised \\ge 60 \\mid Exercised \\ge 40) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{# participants who exercised at least [math]40[/math] and at least [math]60[/math]}{# participants who exercised at least [math]40[/math]} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{# participants who exercised at least [math]60[/math]}{# participants who exercised at least [math]40[/math]}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe total number of participants who exercised at least $40$ minutes is the sum of the participants in the $40 - 59$, $60 - 79$, and $80 - 100$ groups:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}12 + 9 + 4} = {\\color{blue}25}\n$$\n\n| Workout Duration (minutes) | Number of Participants |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0-19$ | $5$ |\n| $20-39$ | $8$ |\n| $40-59$ | $\\color{blue}12$ |\n| $60-79$ | $\\color{blue}9$ |\n| $80-100$ | $\\color{blue}4$ |\n\nOn the other hand, the number of participants who exercised at least $60$ minutes is the sum of the participants in the $60 - 79$ and $80 - 100$ groups:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}9} + {\\color{red}4} = {\\color{red}13}\n$$\n\n| Workout Duration (minutes) | Number of Participants |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0-19$ | $5$ |\n| $20-39$ | $8$ |\n| $40-59$ | $12$ |\n| $60-79$ | $\\color{red}9$ |\n| $80-100$ | $\\color{red}4$ |\n\nTherefore, the fraction of participants who exercised at least $60$ minutes out of those who exercised at least $40$ minutes is\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Exercised} \\geq 60 \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Exercised} \\geq 40) = \\dfrac{\\color{red}13}{\\color{blue}25}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** A common mistake is dividing by the grand total instead of the conditional group. So, we always identify the condition first, then narrow down to the event." :tutorial/id #uuid "59209c70-3cff-591a-a7dd-8c69ce25ebd3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13590 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231983 :tutorial/content "Previously, we considered the differential equation $$4xy'' + 2y' - y = 0,$$ and, using the Frobenius trial solution $$y=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^{\\lambda+n},$$ we found that the possible values of $\\lambda$ are $\\lambda=0$ and $\\lambda=\\dfrac12.$ Since $\\lambda\\in(0,1),$ we take $\\lambda=\\dfrac12.$\n\nLet's now determine a **recurrence relation** for the coefficients of a Frobenius series solution of our previous example, for the case $\\lambda \\in (0,1).$\n\nRewriting each term of the ODE as a power series (with the $\\color{blue}n=0$ term isolated), we obtained $$\\begin{aligned}4x{y}^{''} & =4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 2{y}^{'} & =2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -y & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe continue from this point.\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nSince we already handled the ${\\color{blue}n=0}$ terms in the previous slide to obtain the indicial equation, we focus only on the coefficients of the $n\\geq 1$ terms here.\n\nFor $n\\geq 1,$ collecting like powers of $x$ gives $$\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}-{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}-{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & = \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(4(\\lambda +n)^{2}-2(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}-{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & .\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 5**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nFor each $n\\geq 1,$ the coefficient of $x^n$ must be $0,$ so $$\\begin{aligned}(4(\\lambda +n)^{2}-2(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}-{a}_{n-1} & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +n)(4(\\lambda +n)-2){a}_{n}-{a}_{n-1} & =0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, since we are working in the case $\\lambda\\in(0,1),$ we substitute $\\lambda=\\dfrac12$ and solve for $a_n{:}$ $$\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{2}+n)(4(\\frac{1}{2}+n)-2){a}_{n} & ={a}_{n-1} \\\\ 4n(n+\\frac{1}{2}){a}_{n} & ={a}_{n-1} \\\\ 2n(2n+1){a}_{n} & ={a}_{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2n(2n+1)},\\,n\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c8268534-e946-58bc-bba7-05c05e081ac1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14710 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232008 :tutorial/content "A **linear expression** is an algebraic expression that contains a variable, but the variable is only multiplied by a number or divided by a number.\n\nSome examples of linear expressions are $3n+5$,\n\n$$\nx-\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y}{5}+2\n$$\n\nTo **evaluate** an algebraic expression, we replace the variable with the value that it represents and then simplify.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the expression $3n+5$ and we know that $n=6$.\nThen $n$ represents the number $6$, so we replace $n$ with $6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3n + 5 &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot n + 5 &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 6 + 5 &=  \\\\\n18 + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 23 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when $n=6$, the value of the expression $3n+5$ is $23$." :tutorial/id #uuid "26c98f1c-d032-527d-87b5-1d15c95b861d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14850 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232009 :tutorial/content "To evaluate an algebraic expression, we replace the variable with a given value and then simplify the resulting numerical expression.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate the expression $2x^2$ when $x=3$. This means we replace $x$ with $3$ everywhere it appears.\n\nAfter replacing the variable, we simplify using the order of operations, so exponents are evaluated before multiplication and addition. In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} &=  \\\\\n2 \\cdot 3^{2} &=  \\\\\n2 \\cdot 9 &=  \\\\\n&= 18 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen an expression contains an exponent, remember that the exponent tells us how many times the base is multiplied by itself. For instance, $3^2$ means $3\\times3$, and $3^3$ means $3\\times3\\times3$.\n\nIn this lesson, you will practice evaluating expressions by carefully replacing variables with given values and simplifying step by step." :tutorial/id #uuid "587f93fc-7040-52f6-ac08-ad6fb52ca83a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14851 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232010 :tutorial/content "When simplifying a linear expression, our goal is to combine like terms so that the expression has fewer terms and is easier to work with.\n\nRecall that like terms are terms that have the same variable part. Because the variable parts represent the same quantity, the terms can be added or subtracted.\n\nFor example, consider the expression $8x - 3x$. The terms $8x$ and $-3x$ are like terms, so we can combine them by adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x - 3x &=  \\\\\n\\underbrace{(8 - 3)}_{\\text{coefficients}}x &=  \\\\\n&= 5x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen combining like terms:\n\n- add the coefficients if the terms are being added, and\n- subtract the coefficients if one term is being subtracted.\n\nIn this lesson, you will practice simplifying expressions by combining like terms step by step.\n\nSometimes, a term may appear without a written number in front of the variable. For example, $x$ means we are adding $1x$, and $-x$ means we are subtracting $1x$." :tutorial/id #uuid "79f4710f-b4fb-52da-9771-ede25d3f2877" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14860 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232011 :tutorial/content "The **distributive law** describes how multiplication works when it is applied to a sum inside parentheses.\n\nWhen a number is multiplied by a sum, the multiplication is **distributed** to each term in the sum. In other words, the number outside the parentheses multiplies every term inside the parentheses.\n\nFor example, consider the expression $3(2x + 5)$. The number $3$ multiplies both terms inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(2x + 5) &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 2x + 3 \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 6x + 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter applying the distributive law, the parentheses are removed, and the expression is written as a sum of the resulting terms.\n\nIn this lesson, you will learn how to apply the distributive law to different linear expressions." :tutorial/id #uuid "49b67f5c-181d-5253-8e62-cb61c7e7e48b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14862 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232012 :tutorial/content "The distributive law can also be used when the expression inside the parentheses is a *difference*.\n\nWhen a number is multiplied by an expression with a minus sign, the number outside the parentheses still multiplies every term inside the parentheses. The subtraction sign stays between the terms.\n\nFor example, consider the expression $5(4x - 3)$. The number $5$ multiplies both $4x$ and $3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(4x - 3) &=  \\\\\n5 \\cdot 4x - 5 \\cdot 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 20x - 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter distributing, the parentheses are removed, and the expression is written as the difference of the resulting terms." :tutorial/id #uuid "cf84ec04-f4eb-57e3-9547-b2d79d813041" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14863 :tutorial/title "Dealing With a Minus Inside the Parentheses"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232019 :tutorial/content "Two expressions are **equivalent** if they always have the same value, even if they look different.\n\nA useful way to check whether two expressions are equivalent is to simplify each one. If they can be written in the same form, they are equivalent. This often involves using techniques like *distributing*, *collecting like terms*, or *factoring*.\n\nFor example, let's check if $2(3x+4)$ and $8x+8-2x$ are equivalent.\n\nThe first expression, $2(3x+4)$, is already simplified in a factored form. We will now simplify the second expression, $8x+8-2x$, to see if we can get it into the same form.\n\nFirst, we simplify the second expression by collecting like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x + 8 - 2x &=  \\\\\n8x - 2x + 8 &=  \\\\\n(8 - 2)x + 8 &=  \\\\\n&= 6x + 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor out the greatest common factor. Since\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(6x,8)=2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x + 8 &=  \\\\\n(2 \\cdot 3x) + (2 \\cdot 4) &=  \\\\\n&= 2(3x + 4) \\mid.✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe simplified form, $2(3x+4)$, matches the first expression.\n\nSince the expressions can be written in the same form, they are equivalent." :tutorial/id #uuid "c14a5fb5-5b73-55bd-8168-4faaf9dd97c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14881 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232021 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, like terms are not written next to each other. In these cases, we first rearrange the expression so that like terms are grouped together, and then we combine them.\n\nFor example, consider the expression $12r - 5 + 3r$. The two variable terms, $12r$ and $3r$, are like terms, but they are separated by a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the expression so that the like terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12r - 5 + 3r &=  \\\\\n&= \\underbrace{12r + 3r}_{r\\text{ terms}} - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the like terms are grouped, we combine them by adding their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\underbrace{12r + 3r} - 5 &=  \\\\\n(12 + 3)r - 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 15r - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging terms makes it easier to see which terms can be combined before simplifying the expression." :tutorial/id #uuid "8b887afb-7d8f-543c-abcb-514c964cf44f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14914 :tutorial/title "Rearranging Terms to Group Like Terms"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232022 :tutorial/content "Some linear expressions contain terms with different variables. Even though these terms may look similar, they are **not** like terms and cannot be combined with each other.\n\nFor example, consider the expression $6x - 2x + 5y + y$. The $x$ terms and the $y$ terms represent different quantities, so they must be combined separately.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - 2x + 5y + y &=  \\\\\n&= \\underbrace{6x - 2x}_{x\\text{ terms}} + \\underbrace{5y + y}_{y\\text{ terms}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now combine each group of like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\underbrace{6x - 2x}_{} + \\underbrace{5y + y}_{} &=  \\\\\n(6 - 2)x + (5 + 1)y &=  \\\\\n&= 4x + 6y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen simplifying expressions with more than one variable, always group and combine like terms one variable at a time." :tutorial/id #uuid "78aa92f9-f4b8-53be-a338-c9c2c6897d67" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14915 :tutorial/title "Simplifying Expressions with More Than One Variable"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232023 :tutorial/content "In real-world situations, algebraic expressions are often used to model quantities such as distance, cost, or time. Evaluating the expression means replacing the variable with a given value and interpreting the result in the context of the situation.\n\nSuppose the total cost (in dollars) of renting a bike for $t$ hours is given by the expression $12t + 5$. The value $5$ represents a fixed fee, and $12t$ represents the hourly cost.\n\nTo find the total cost of renting the bike for $3$ hours, we replace $t$ with $3$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12t + 5 &=  \\\\\n12 \\cdot 3 + 5 &=  \\\\\n36 + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 41 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe numerical result tells us the value of the quantity, and the context tells us how to interpret it. In this case, the total cost of renting the bike for $3$ hours is $\\[math]41$.\n\nWhen evaluating expressions in real-world contexts, always remember to\n\n- replace the variable with the given value,\n- simplify using the order of operations, and\n- interpret the final value using the appropriate units." :tutorial/id #uuid "9a3176a5-4497-54fc-b816-ab06d3cb9d55" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14916 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Expressions in Real-World Contexts"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232032 :tutorial/content "A **one-step division equation** is an equation like\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{5}=7\n$$\n\nwhere the variable is divided by a number.\n\nTo solve a one-step division equation, we isolate the variable by reversing the operation that is applied to it.\n\nFor example, in the equation above, the variable $x$ is *divided* by $5$. The opposite of dividing by $5$ is *multiplying* by $5$.\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x}{5} &= 7 \\\\\n\\frac{x}{5} \\cdot 5 &= 7 \\cdot 5 \\\\\nx &= 35\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis isolates the variable, so we have found the solution.\n\n**Note**: We can see how multiplying by the denominator removes it from the left-hand side by swapping denominators and simplifying:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x}{5} \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n\\frac{x}{1} \\cdot \\frac{5}{5} &=  \\\\\nx \\cdot 1 &=  \\\\\n&= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that if the variable appears on the right-hand side $\\Big($e.g.,\n\n$$\n7=\\dfrac{x}5\\Big)\n$$\n\nyou may first swap the left and right sides of the equation to get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}5=7\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bbedbed2-eef7-53a4-9a9c-98c6cf799230" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14976 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232047 :tutorial/content "Recall the following length conversions:\n\n- $1\\,\\textrm{ft} = 12\\,\\textrm{in}$\n- $1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$\n\nWhen we convert from a smaller unit to a larger unit, we may need to use long division to divide by the number of smaller units in one larger unit.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $84$ feet into yards.\n\nWe start with the unit conversion between yards and feet:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nSince we are converting from feet to yards, we divide both sides of the equation by $3$ and obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd} = 1\\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nWe want to determine how many yards are in $84$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $84{:}$\n\nFinally, we simplify this fraction by interpreting\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{84}{3}\n$$\n\nas the division $84 \\div 3$. We can calculate this using long division:\n\n|  | $2$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |  |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $6$ |  |\n|  | $2$ | $4$ |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n|  |  | $\\fbox{0}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n84\\,\\textrm{ft} = 28\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will use long division to simplify the quotients that appear when converting measurements to larger units." :tutorial/id #uuid "acaf1894-f7cb-5d8c-b88d-97fb846d6f5b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15192 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232048 :tutorial/content "Sometimes we need two conversions to change a measurement into a larger unit.\n\nFor example, to convert inches to yards, we can convert\n\n$$\n\\textrm{inches} \\rightarrow \\textrm{feet} \\rightarrow \\textrm{yards}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $360$ inches into yards. We first convert $360$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $360$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{ft} = 12\\,\\textrm{in}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\,\\textrm{ft} = 1\\,\\textrm{in}$We want to determine how many feet are in $360$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $360{:}$$360 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft|= 360 \\cdot 1in; \\frac{360}{12}ft|= 360in$Now, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{360}{12}$ as the division $360 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:$3$$0$$12$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$0$$0$Therefore,$360\\,\\textrm{in} = 30\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n- Next, to convert from $30$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd} = 1\\,\\textrm{ft}$We want to determine how many yards are in $30$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $30{:}$$30 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd|= 30 \\cdot 1ft; \\frac{30}{3}yd|= 30ft; 10yd|= 30ft$Therefore,$30\\,\\textrm{ft} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion,\n\n$$\n360\\,\\textrm{in} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nTo convert inches to yards, we convert inches to feet first, then convert feet to yards." :tutorial/id #uuid "48e375bd-70b2-58b8-9f53-dd21756f2f20" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15195 :tutorial/title "Two-Step Length Conversions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232054 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose $h=35$. Let's compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{h}7\n$$\n\nAs before, replacing $h$ with $35$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{h}{7} &=  \\\\\nh \\div 7 &=  \\\\\n35 \\div 7 &=  \\\\\n&= 5 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "af128711-923c-57c7-8fea-13f16a069b13" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15343 :tutorial/title "Expressions With Multiplication and Division"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232055 :tutorial/content "We can combine several arithmetic operations in linear expressions.\n\nFor example, given that $m = 6$, let's compute $2m + 5$.\n\nReplacing $m$ with $6$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2m + 5 &=  \\\\\n2 \\cdot 6 + 5 &=  \\\\\n12 + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 17 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "43648158-2cb2-5944-8113-008f48a7434c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15344 :tutorial/title "Combining Different Operations in Expressions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232056 :tutorial/content "Finally, let's see how we can evaluate linear expressions in context.\n\nSuppose the temperature (in degrees Celsius) after $t$ hours of a liquid mixture as it cools is given by the expression\n$38 - 4t$.\nWhat is the temperature of the mixture after\n\n$$\n\\dfrac52\n$$\n\nhours?\n\nTo determine the temperature after\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nhours, we replace $t$ with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac52\n$$\n\nand evaluate the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n38 - 4t &=  \\\\\n38 - 4 \\cdot t &=  \\\\\n38 - \\frac{4}{1} \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} &=  \\\\\n38 - \\frac{4}{2} \\cdot \\frac{5}{1} &=  \\\\\n38 - 2 \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n38 - 10 &=  \\\\\n&= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature after\n\n$$\n\\dfrac52\n$$\n\nhours is $28\\,^\\circ\\textrm{C}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "24a55d2e-53c7-5fd7-9c6e-d8522f61dca4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15345 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Linear Expressions in Context"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489385 :tutorial/content "A rational number can have a decimal representation that either *ends* or *repeats*.\n\nIn this lesson, we will see how to convert rational numbers written as fractions into decimals using *long division*. \nWe begin with examples where the decimal terminates. \n\nLet's convert $\\dfrac{7}{2}$ into a decimal using long division.\n\nTo write the fraction as a decimal, we divide the numerator by the denominator:\n\n$$\n7 \\div 2\n$$\n\nWe proceed using long division as usual, stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"2\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,3    {\\, . \\,} 5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"2\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7   {\\, . \\,} 0}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{2} = 3.5.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6c539020-aca7-5b8f-a2b0-b72c56336242" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15179 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489388 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following rational number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nTo find its decimal representation, we divide $1$ by $3$ using long division, as follows:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"4\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,0 \\,. 3 {\\:\\phantom{|}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{|}} 3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\:\\ldots$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"4\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\, . \\,} 0  {\\:\\phantom{|}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{|}} 0}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\ldots$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNotice that, in this case,\n\n- the long division process never ends, and\n\n- the digit $3$ after the decimal point repeats *forever*.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13 = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nThis is an example of a **repeating decimal** - a decimal in which the digits after the decimal point continue forever in a *repeating pattern*." :tutorial/id #uuid "b877953a-1821-5fae-aebf-3ff936971679" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15177 :tutorial/title "Repeating Decimals"}]}
